## Follow-on Stretch Goals (Optional)

### Exported Standalone Library Test Suite

* `excel_grapher.CodeGenerator` programmatically generates a differential test suite along with the standalone Python library. Functionality for exporting tests does not currently exist in `excel-grapher`, so this would be a new `CodeGenerator` feature.

### Exported Function to Set Inputs from Filled-out Excel Template

* The standalone library should support either setting inputs manually or loading them from a filled-out Excel template. I prototyped this in `Teal-Insights/lic-dsf-extraction-pipeline`, but I can't remember whether it's directly incorporated into `excel-grapher`'s export functionality yet. If not, we should add this functionality as an export option so it doesn't have to be added in post-processing.

### Refactored Standalone Library Internals

* The team should analyze the graph to identify: 1. connected parts of the graph that should be functions/modules, and 2. identical parts of the graph that should be deduplicated. `excel-grapher` provides some tooling for this (specifically, a NetworkX layout plugin intended to support module inference), but this is mostly not yet implemented and will require experimentation by the team. Whatever process we develop for module inference must be scalable to larger graphs.
* The final refactored library should still pass differential testing against Excel, but should be significantly reduced in code size. Library internals should be well organized into functions that map to macrofinance computations, not individual Excel workbook cells. Library internals should have their own docstrings.

### Library Publication from CI/CD

* In `Teal-Insights/lic-dsf-extraction-pipeline`, I prototyped generating and publishing the standalone library directly from GitHub Actions. The *pipeline* repository pushes to a different *production* repository that contains the published library for download by end users.

## Execution Plan

### AI Usage

Each contributor should read the others' code. Christopher reads Kweku's harness; Kweku reads Christopher's extraction; Teal reads both.

We use AI where it's strong (multi-step technical planning, dependency reasoning, decomposition of complex problems) and inject human domain expertise where AI is weak (calendar estimation under AI-agent pace, sovereign-debt guild knowledge that isn't written down, and the verification that best practices Council recommends are actually executed and not faked).

**Council of experts loop**: AI produces clarifying questions, humans provide answers, AI proposes a plan and spec, humans and other AI models provide adversarial review of spec and plan, then iterate until shared human/AI sign-off. Every artifact and every decision in this loop gets attribution. SpecStory CLI captures agent-interaction records per-author; git commit history captures repo state; planning-repo notes capture human reasoning; a manual-intervention log captures the bridging moments.

### Three Lanes

**The three lanes are extraction (Christopher), golden-master testing (Kweku), and economic-logic design (Teal).** The sign-off pattern is layered. Teal signs off on economic meaning, whether the interface speaks to a domain expert. Christopher certifies software correctness, that the generated code honors the library API contract and is well-engineered. Kweku signs off on extraction parity — that the extracted Python passes the harness at the locked precision tolerance and that root-cause analysis surfaces no fundamental gaps.

This allows for work ("actions") to be performed in parallel. Deliverables pass between lanes via "handoffs" at "approval gates". Handoffs result in either approval or an iterative feedback loop until approval is reached. In some cases, we may find flaws in a deliverable and decide to proceed with approval anyway. In that case, feedback should still be passed back to the author of the deliverable and documented by the author for future action.

### What This Looks Like in Practice

We can think in terms of three abstractions for coordination: **action**, **handoff**, and **approval gate**. We've already set the precedent during our planning process. Drafting and synthesizing are actions, peer reviews/signoffs are approval gates, and handoffs connect them to each other:

```
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Christopher
  participant K as Kweku
  participant T as Teal

  par Initial drafting
    C->>T: Draft input for plan of action
  and
    K->>T: Draft input for plan of action
  and
    T->>T: Draft own input
  end

  T->>T: Synthesize unified plan draft

  loop Until both approvals are yes
    T->>C: Handoff synthesized draft for approval
    C-->>T: Approve? (Yes / No with revisions)

    T->>K: Handoff synthesized draft for approval
    K-->>T: Approve? (Yes / No with revisions)

    alt C or K says No
      T->>T: Incorporate revisions and resynthesize
    else Both say Yes
      T-->>C: Final approved plan
      T-->>K: Final approved plan
    end
  end
```

Here we have three lanes, with the labeled "loop" representing an approval gate. Outside of an approval gate, work is loosely coupled and can be performed in parallel. At the seams (approval gates), there are sequential dependencies where work in one lane may be blocked until a handoff from another lane occurs.

With twelve approval gates (although not every gate involves handoffs), our challenge is going to be managing those bottlenecks effectively. Each team member will need to be accountable to provide fast feedback to keep the loop moving, and team members should also feel free to jump ahead to work on the next deliverable when work is blocked by a handoff that hasn't occurred yet.

